¶
'''unshamefastly†''' (un-shām′fȧst-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''unschamefastly''; < ''unshamefast'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Without shame; boldly. ''Wyclif'', Prov. xxi. 29.

'''unshamefastness†''' (un-shām′fȧst-nes), ''n.'' The state of being unshamefast; impudence.
{{smaller block|We haue not wanted this Lent fishe to eate, and also sinnes ynow to confesse; for the case is come to suche dissolution and ''unshamefastnesse'' that the gentlemen hold it for an estate and aduancement of honour to eate flesh in Lent. ''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 85.}}

'''unshape''' (un-shāp′), ''v. t.'' To deprive of shape; throw out of form or into disorder; confound; derange. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|This deed ''unshapes'' me quite. ''Shak.'', M. for M., iv. 4. 23.}}

'''unshapen''' (un-shā′pn), ''a.'' Shapeless; misshapen; deformed; ugly.
{{smaller block|Thou wild ''unshapen'' antic.
{{right|''Middleton and Rowley'', Changeling, iv. 3.}}}}

'''unshapely''' (un-shāp′li), ''a.'' Not shapely; not well-formed; ill-formed.
{{smaller block|Metaphysics reared many an apparently-solid edifice, which fell into ''unshapely'' ruin at the first rude blast of criticism. ''J. Fiske'', Cosmic Philos., I. 26.}}

'''unshared''' (un-shãrd′), ''a.'' Not shared; not partaken or enjoyed in common: as, ''unshared'' bliss. ''Milton'', P. L., ix. 880.

'''unshave†''' (un-shāv′), ''a.'' Unshaven. ''Surrey'', Æneid, iv.

'''unshaven''' (un-shā′vn), ''a.'' Not shaven; untrimmed.

'''unsheathe''' (un-shēᴛ̵ʜ′), ''v.'' '''I.''' ''trans.'' To draw from the sheath or scabbard. ''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., ii. 2. 123.—'''To unsheathe the sword''', {{smaller|figuratively, to make war.}}
